<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="reading-question-de-morgans-laws" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    De Morgan's laws
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    De Morgan's laws
  </idx>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> S \setminus \of{ T \cap U } = \of{ S \setminus T } \cup \of{ S \setminus U } </m> for all sets <m> S </m>, <m> T </m>, and <m> U </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        First let <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cap U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S </m> and <m> x \notin T \cap U </m>. We want to show that <m> x \in S \setminus T </m> or <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>. To that end, suppose that <m> x \notin S \setminus T </m>. Since <m> x \in S </m>, we must have <m> x \in T </m>. 
      </p>
      
      <p>
        But <m> x \notin T \cap U </m>, and so <m> x \notin U </m>; that is, <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>. Since if <m> x \notin S \setminus T </m>, then <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>, we conclude that <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cup \of{ S \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now let <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cup \of{ S \setminus U } </m>, so that <m> x \in S \setminus T </m> or <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>. If <m> x \in S \setminus T </m>, then <m> x \in S </m> and <m> x \notin T </m>, so that <m> x \notin T \cap U </m>. We conclude that <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cap U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cap U } </m> if and only if <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cup \of{ S \setminus U } </m>. We conclude that <m> S \setminus \of{ T \cap U } = \of{ S \setminus T } \cup \of{ S \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> S \setminus \of{ T \cup U } = \of{ S \setminus T } \cap \of{ S \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        First let <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cup U } </m>. Then <m> x \in S </m> and <m> x \notin T \cup U </m>. If <m> x \in T </m>, then <m> x \in T \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \notin T </m>; we conclude that <m> x \in S \setminus T </m>. Similarly, if <m> x \in U </m>, then <m> x \in T \cup U </m>, and so <m> x \notin U </m>; we conclude that <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>. Thus <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cap \of{ S \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now let <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cap \of{ S \setminus U } </m>, so that <m> x \in S \setminus T </m> and <m> x \in S \setminus U </m>. Thus <m> x \in S </m>, <m> x \notin T </m>, and <m> x \notin U </m>. If <m> x \in T \cup U </m>, then <m> x \in T </m> or <m> x \in U </m>, and so <m> x \notin T \cup U </m>; we conclude that <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cup U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in S \setminus \of{ T \cup U } </m> if and only if <m> x \in \of{ S \setminus T } \cap \of{ S \setminus U } </m>. We conclude that <m> S \setminus \of{ T \cup U } = \of{ S \setminus T } \cap \of{ S \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Taken together, these results are called <term> De Morgan's laws </term>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</exercise>
